CONTEXT. This page belongs to the bundle on Abelian functions and theta functions. It is a working sheet on the genus p=3 case: the 28 bitangents of a plane quartic and the 28 odd theta characteristics. The previous and next folios carry the same tables and the same normal form, √(xξ)+√(yη)+√(zζ)=0 (written out on the next page). Here x,y,z,ξ,η,ζ are linear forms in three homogeneous variables, subject to linear relations of the type αx+βy+γz+ξ/α+η/β+ζ/γ=0. That relation is written explicitly on the next page; a primed analogue is also used.

(A) TABLE OF CHARACTERISTICS (upper left; corrected from the image). Each square root of a bitangent form gets a 2x3 binary characteristic [g;h]:
- √x=[111;111], √ξ=[100;100], √y=[010;010], √η=[001;001], √z=[100;111], √ζ=[111;100];
- √(ξ+η+ζ)=[010;111], √(x+y+z)=[111;010], √(αx+βy+γz)=[001;111], √(ξ/α+η/β+ζ/γ)=[111;001];
- √(ξ/α+βy+γz)=[110;011], √(αx+η/β+γz)=[011;110], √(αx+βy+ζ/γ)=[101;011], plus a further form = [011;101];
- the primed row: [001;011], [100;110], [010;011], [100;101].

Every listed characteristic is odd, i.e. g·h ≡ 1 mod 2 (checked by me for all 18). The pair sums are x+ξ = y+η = z+ζ = [011;011]. These three pairs therefore lie in one Steiner complex, which is exactly what is needed for √(xξ)+√(yη)+√(zζ)=0 to be the curve. A small mod-2 column sum below the table ('... = 0') is a check of this kind of additive relation.

(B) SEARCH FOR OTHER RIEMANN-TYPE EQUATIONS. Riemann writes many trial expressions of the form √(pP) ± √(qQ) ± √(rR). Examples:
- √(xη) − √(yζ) + √((ξ+η+ζ)(x+y+z));
- √(xη) − √(zξ) + √(...);
- √(x(ξ+η+ζ)) + √(y(x+y+z)) + √(z(x+y+z)) and sign variants;
- the scaled versions √(αx·η/β) − √(βy·ξ/α) + √((ξ/α+η/β+ζ/γ)(αx+βy+γz)), and √(αxβy) − √(ξη/(αβ)) + ....

Such an expression can vanish identically on the quartic only if the three pairs have the same characteristic sum. Two cases do work:
- x+η = ξ+y = (αx+βy+γz)+(ξ/α+η/β+ζ/γ) = [110;110]. This gives √((α/β)xη) − √((β/α)yξ) + √((αx+βy+γz)(ξ/α+η/β+ζ/γ)), which appears on the next page.
- x+y = ξ+η = (x+y+z)+(ξ+η+ζ) = [101;101]. This gives √(xy) − √(ξη) + √((x+y+z)(ξ+η+ζ)), present here in scaled form and on the next page.

Several other trials (e.g. with yζ) fail this test, and some are struck out.

(C) TEST VIA CONTACT POINTS. Riemann restricts to the line x+y+z=0 and requires √(xζ/(ξz)) = √(xη/(yξ)) = √(yζ/(zη)) = 1, i.e. x/ξ = y/η = z/ζ (=t²). Then √(xξ)+√(yη)+√(zζ) = t(ξ+η+ζ) while x+y+z = t²(ξ+η+ζ). This is a check that the given line touches the curve doubly, i.e. is a bitangent.

(D) NEW LINEAR FORMS BY ELIMINATION (top and right column). Riemann rewrites combinations using the linear relations. For example:
- αx/(β/γ−1) + (y/β)/(1/(αγ)−1) + γz/(β/α−1);
- ((β−α)/γ)/(β/γ−1)·x + ((1/(γα)−1/(γβ))/(1/(γα)−1))·y + z;
- x/(1−β/γ) + y/(1−αγ) + z/(1−α/β), and ξ/(α(βγ−1)) + y/(α−γ) + z/(α−β).

Simplified (my step): (β/γ−α/γ)/(β/γ−1) = (β−α)/(β−γ), and (1/(γα)−1/(γβ))/(1/(γα)−1) = (β−α)/(β(1−αγ)). The aim is to express the remaining bitangents, e.g. the one with characteristic [110;101], as explicit forms. The middle lines (α−1/γ)((x+y+z)(1−β/γ) + x(α/γ−1) + ...) = α(β−1/γ)(α−1/γ), and similarly for β, are elimination identities of the same kind; they are partly illegible.

(E) TRANSFORMED NORMAL FORM. Riemann posits a new relation of the same shape for a different sextuple of bitangents:
α'²x + y/α'² + β'²(·) + η/β'² + γ'²(x+y+z) + (ξ+η+ζ)/γ'² = 0.
He then solves for the new parameters in terms of α,β,γ, in several attempts with differing signs:
- β'²/α'² = −β/α;
- γ'²/α'² = −(α/γ−1)/(β/γ−1), or its reciprocal form in the first attempt;
- α'²γ'² = ±(α−1/γ)/(β−1/γ);
- β'²γ'² = (β/α)(1−1/(γα))/(1−1/(γβ)).
Multiplying gives α'²β'² = −(β/α)·((αγ−1)/(βγ−1))·((α−γ)/(β−γ)), as written.

(F) LOWER PART. An attempt to derive the curve equation in the new form. Starting from √(xζ) = −√(yη) + √(zξ), he forms combinations such as (γ−1/α)(γ−α)√(xζ) + (γ−β)(γ−1/β)√(yη) and expands coefficients. These lines are partly struck out and not completed on this page.
